sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, single-swamp-node, agent-observer-swamp-acts, root-of-trust-gateway-only, tool.manager_converge removed (shared Tool Manager setup step). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#add-a-node.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 17, 22 (cfgmgmt apt/user/fetch/file/systemd/authorized_key; Q-WGHUB hub config as file + wg syncconf; DNS record kept: a Node name is not covered by a TLD answer).
  %% Standalone Mac inspection runs on role=swamp: Read Mac platform, Read Mac addresses, Inspect the Mac, Check the Mac. It checks no agent or runtime ownership.
  %% Standalone bootstrap probe also runs on role=swamp, using its admitted SSH target and vault identity.
  %% The native parent owns enrollment, role children and the terminal check in one run.
  %% status: migrated
  %% command: node:add
  %% kind: node.add
  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as New Node 12<br/>reached over ssh
  participant HUB as WireGuard hub<br/>vpn Node 1
  CLI->>GW: node:add beast 203.0.113.7<br/>--host-key-fingerprint=…
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:add beast 203.0.113.7 --host-key-fingerprint=SHA256:…`
  %%> - syntax: `<name> [host] [--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256] [--ssh-port=PORT] [--user=USER] [--orbit-user=USER] [--platform=linux|macos] [--architecture=ARCH] [--tld=TLD] [--role=ROLE]… [--cluster=ID] [--wireguard-ip=IP] [--lan-ip=IP] [--wireguard-endpoint=HOST:PORT] [--dns-server=IP] [--setting=PATH:VALUE]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node name and its public SSH host
  %%> - the approved SSH host key fingerprint; required for a new Node
  %%> - the host and the fingerprint are required for a new Node; everything else is optional
  %%> Optional flags:
  %%> - `--role=ROLE` (repeatable): adds the role steps in `opt --role`
  %%> - `--platform=macos`: skips the Linux steps and adds `opt --platform=macos`
  %%> - `--tld`, `--cluster`, `--ssh-port`, `--user`, `--orbit-user`, `--architecture`, `--wireguard-ip`, `--lan-ip`, `--wireguard-endpoint`, `--dns-server`, `--setting`: recorded in the intent; they add no steps
  %%> - `--json`: machine-readable output
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - adds the machine to the fleet as Node 12, named `beast`
  %%> - for a Node that already exists, the same command converges it again with its new TLD, roles or settings
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, record Node 12<br/>provisioning · task #6201
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the name is free, or names a Node that owns no Instances (`node.has_instances`)
  %%> - a new Node has a host and a host key fingerprint (`node.ssh_host_fingerprint_required`)
  %%> - Linux accepts only `x86_64`; `app-dev` needs a TLD, unless its Cluster has one
  %%> - macOS names one existing account in `--user` and `--orbit-user`, an existing WireGuard address, and no role
  %%> - admission records one immutable resource plan; completed Node work stays on a failed run
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the WireGuard address `10.6.0.42` and the hub peer entry
  %%> - base packages, managed account, native sudo rule and public SSH rule
  %%> - the Orbit SSH block, the pinned `orbit-agent` release and the Node agent configuration
  %%> - the role steps for each requested role
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Node 12 recorded as `provisioning`; an existing Node keeps its record until the result
  %%> - task #6201 recorded with the full intent
  %%> - nothing has happened on the machine yet
  GW->>SW: Push keys to vault<br/>bootstrap-identity-node-12 · agent
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the first login uses the bootstrap SSH identity, a private key that only the Gateway holds
  %%> - the Node agent needs a new secret, and secrets travel only through the vault (R3)
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - pushes Node 12's bootstrap identity as vault key `bootstrap-identity-node-12`
  %%> - pushes Node 12's new Node agent secret as vault key `agent-secret-node-12`
  %%> Never:
  %%> - a fixed key name for every Node, or a key value in the run inputs
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the run reads both keys from the vault; certificate signing uses the existing Gateway signer
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-add
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the host, port, bootstrap user, managed user and approved fingerprint
  %%> - packages, pins, Node agent configuration and frozen role resources
  %%> - the hub's address and SSH host keys, and the new peer entry
  %%> - secrets only as vault key names
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation #6201
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  %% Every Swamp lane label is an actual step in operation-node-add.
  SW->>N: Read the host key<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Require approved host key<br/>native assert
  SW->>N: Read the platform<br/>SSH argv
  %%> Linux by default:
  %%> - `--platform` defaults to `linux`, so the Linux steps are the main path
  %%> - the workflow guards each Linux step on the admitted platform (`inputs.intent.platform == "linux"`), not on what this step observes
  %%> - with `--platform=macos` those steps are skipped and `opt --platform=macos` runs instead
  SW->>N: Read the bootstrap account<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Bind the Node identity<br/>public resource binding
  SW->>N: Read bootstrap keys<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Authorize the control key<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Remove an old host pin<br/>control Node SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Pin the bootstrap host<br/>control Node SSH argv
  SW->>N: Refresh base packages<br/>cfgmgmt apt
  SW->>N: Install base packages<br/>cfgmgmt apt
  SW->>N: Create the managed account<br/>cfgmgmt user
  SW->>N: Stage the sudo rule<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Validate the sudo rule<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Publish the sudo rule<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Write the Orbit keys<br/>cfgmgmt authorized_key
  SW->>N: Open public recovery SSH<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Enable the firewall<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Install the Node agent<br/>cfgmgmt fetch
  SW->>N: Create WireGuard folder<br/>cfgmgmt directory
  SW->>N: Prepare the WireGuard key<br/>cfgmgmt exec
  SW->>N: Read WireGuard public key<br/>cfgmgmt debug_exec
  SW->>SW: Require unique tunnel key<br/>native assert
  SW->>N: Write the tunnel files<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Enable hub forwarding<br/>cfgmgmt sysctl
  SW->>N: Start the tunnel<br/>cfgmgmt systemd
  SW->>N: Set the tunnel address<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Remove the retired address<br/>cfgmgmt exec
  SW->>N: Stage the tunnel key<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Finish tunnel config<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Apply the tunnel peers<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Trust WireGuard members<br/>SSH argv
  opt Linux peer joins the VPN hub
  SW->>HUB: Create the hub folders<br/>cfgmgmt directory
  SW->>HUB: Write hub configuration<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>HUB: Stage the hub private key<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>HUB: Finish hub configuration<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>HUB: Apply the hub peers<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>HUB: Read the hub public key<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Check the hub key<br/>native assert
  end
  SW->>N: Create observer folders<br/>cfgmgmt directory
  SW->>N: Write observer config<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Write the observer secret<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Start the Node agent<br/>cfgmgmt systemd
  SW->>N: Verify managed SSH<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>N: Verify passwordless sudo<br/>SSH argv
  opt eligible CLI plan
  SW->>N: Publish the Orbit CLI<br/>native child workflow
  end
  opt --role
  SW->>N: Converge each role<br/>native child workflow
  end
  opt new VPN hub needs its DNS listener
  SW->>N: Configure DNS backend<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Install DNS listener<br/>native child workflow
  end
  opt private DNS records need publication
  SW->>N: Publish private DNS<br/>native child workflow
  end
  opt --platform=macos
  SW->>N: Inspect the Mac tunnel<br/>SSH argv
  end
  SW->>N: Check the Node agent<br/>cfgmgmt systemd
  SW->>N: Check the agent binary<br/>cfgmgmt fetch
  SW->>N: Check the observer secret<br/>cfgmgmt file
  SW->>N: Read tunnel handshakes<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>SW: Check the Node<br/>native assert
  opt run completed or failed
  SW->>N: Remove staged sudo rule<br/>cfgmgmt file, best effort
  end
  %%> Secrets:
  %%> - bootstrap SSH and the observer secret use existing execution vault copies
  %%> - WireGuard private keys remain in Node key files; only public keys enter outputs
  %%> Root boundary:
  %%> - roles are frozen child-workflow inputs in this run, with no child Operations
  alt every step passes
    N-->>SW: arch · host key · WG key
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the architecture, the host key and the WireGuard public key
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record Node 12 active<br/>pins · roles · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12 becomes active, with its architecture, SSH host key pins and WireGuard key
    %%> - each converged role becomes active; storage settings are stored
    %%> - task #6201 succeeds
    %%> Next:
    %%> - Metrics exporters start only with `metrics:exporter:enable`
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or mismatch
    %%> When:
    %%> - the host key or architecture differs, the bootstrap or a role fails, the hub refuses its configuration, or the check fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark Node 12 failed<br/>task failed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a new Node 12 stays, marked failed at that step, so you can see it
    %%> - an existing Node keeps its earlier record and stays active
    %%> - task #6201 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> - the hub peer stays; it is harmless and a rerun reuses it
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same `node:add` again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #6201
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
